#!/bin/sh
# Every implementation cites a repository, a paper or an online source, or says
# plainly that it was invented. The rule was set by the operator on 2026-09-20
# after it was skipped twice in one session, and a skill and a memory both failed
# to prevent that, because both depend on being remembered. This does not.
#
# A commit that adds or changes Python must carry, in its message, either:
#   * a source -- an http(s) URL, or an arXiv id such as arXiv:2604.21570, or
#     a github.com/owner/repo reference; or
#   * the literal token INVENTED: followed by what was searched for and why
#     nothing was usable.
#
# To bypass for a genuine non-implementation commit (a rename, a typo, a revert):
#   git commit --no-verify
set -eu

changed=$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACM | grep -E '\.py$' || true)
[ -n "$changed" ] || exit 0

msg=$(cat "${1:-.git/COMMIT_EDITMSG}" 2>/dev/null || true)
# A merge or a revert is not an implementation.
case "$msg" in
  Merge*|Revert*) exit 0 ;;
esac

if printf '%s' "$msg" | grep -Eqi 'https?://|arxiv[:. ]?[0-9]{4}\.[0-9]{4,5}|github\.com/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+/[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+'; then
  exit 0
fi
if printf '%s' "$msg" | grep -q 'INVENTED:'; then
  exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<'MSG'

  pre-commit: this commit changes Python and cites nothing.

  Python touched:
MSG
printf '    %s\n' $changed >&2
cat >&2 <<'MSG'

  Before writing code, look for a repository, a paper or an online source that
  already solves it. Then put the source in the commit message:

      Fix the loop-invariant alignment (arXiv:2604.21570, github.com/owner/repo)

  If you searched and nothing exists, say so and say what you searched for:

      INVENTED: searched for prior art on tiered oracle evidence in program
      verification; the nearest analogue is SLSA's manifest-vs-attestation
      distinction, from a different domain, so this code is ours.

  A rename, a typo fix or a revert is not an implementation: git commit --no-verify

MSG
exit 1
